    "summary": "Scorching Scarlet>Fire, rare Morning Dew, solid first set",
    "blurb": "The show opens with a punchy Feel Like A Stranger and rolls through a tight first set highlighted by Let It Grow and Deal. The second set shines with a scorching Scarlet Begonias → Fire on the Mountain sequence, a rolling Playing In The Band jam and a rare Morning Dew. Overall playing is solid with Jerry’s vocals on point and Brent’s organ driving the jams.",
    "review": "August 31, 1981 at the Aladdin Theater delivered one of those quintessential Vegas nights that Deadheads still talk about. Jerry was in good form, laying down energetic guitar licks and solid vocals on numbers like Candyman and Loser, while Bob’s recovered vocal strength added a warm harmony layer. Brent ripped it up on keyboards, his organ work especially bright during Feel Like A Stranger and the soaring Let It Grow. The first set kept a great vibe from start to finish, but it was the second‑set that truly dazzled – a scorching Scarlet Begonias into Fire on the Mountain followed by an expansive Playing In The Band > Drums > Space journey, capped off with a rare, heartfelt rendition of Morning Dew and a rollicking Johnny B. Goode finale. Across the board the band feels locked in; the setlist flow is song‑rich and the performances are consistently strong, making this show a must‑listen for anyone craving that late‑’80s Dead energy.\n\nFor those looking to dive into the night, the clean Miller soundboard capture (gd81-08-31.sbd.miller.30070.sbeok.flacf) offers the best listening experience – crisp SBD tape with only brief audience patches that don’t detract from the overall clarity. It captures the fire of Scarlet>Fire and the nuance of Morning Dew while preserving the live vibe, making it the definitive version of this memorable Aladdin performance.",
